blurb:
Challenge: What C function does this Windows x64 assembly implement (rcx = arg1, edx = arg2)?

reverse:
    xor     eax, eax          ; i = 0
    lea     r8d, [edx-1]      ; j = n-1
.loop:
    cmp     eax, r8d
    jge     .done
    mov     r9d,  [rcx+rax*4] ; tmp = arr[i]
    mov     r10d, [rcx+r8*4]  ; arr[j]
    mov     [rcx+rax*4], r10d ; arr[i] = arr[j]
    mov     [rcx+r8*4], r9d   ; arr[j] = tmp
    inc     eax
    dec     r8d
    jmp     .loop
.done:
    ret

Answer:
void reverse(int *arr, int n) {
    int i = 0, j = n - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        int tmp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = tmp;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
}

1. rcx/edx are the Windows x64 integer args, so rcx is int *arr and edx is int n; eax/r8d are scratch locals i and j = n-1. 2. Each iteration swaps arr[i] and arr[j] through r9d/r10d as tmp, using rax*4/r8*4 scaled-index addressing for the two int slots. 3. cmp eax, r8d / jge .done is the loop guard while (i < j), and inc eax / dec r8d converge the two pointers each pass -- a classic in-place array-reversal idiom.
